text:
alex: Quick question — running into weird hangs when cancelling streaming requests from the WASM client. Flow: fetch stream -> token refresh triggers -> AbortSignal fired from parent -> some requests never terminate and timers keep running. Anyone seen similar with AbortController + token refresh chains?

maria: yup. sounds like a signal chaining/leak. Are you reusing the same AbortController across retries or creating a new one per request?

alex: reusing a controller on the client instance to try to simplify code (one signal per client). maybe that's dumb.

tj: that's the issue. you generally need a fresh AbortController per logical request. if you need parent-level cancellation, compose signals:```js
const parent = parentSignal; // optional
const ctrl = new AbortController();
if (parent) parent.addEventListener('abort', () => ctrl.abort());
const signal = ctrl.signal;
fetch(url, { signal });
```
This avoids reusing a controller that stays aborted or retains listeners.

sam: additional gotcha for streaming: when you abort, some runtimes (older node-fetch / browsers) leave the underlying stream readable and the response body never fully freed. Make sure to explicitly call `response.body.cancel()` (if available) or drain the stream in the catch.

irina: in our Node SDK we saw a second problem — setTimeout-based token refresh pings firing after abort because we didn't clear timers on abort. attach a listener:```js
signal.addEventListener('abort', () => {
  clearTimeout(refreshTimer);
  doCleanup();
});
```
otherwise timers keep re-scheduling background refresh and keep things alive.

alex: interesting. I'm also compiling to wasm32-unknown-unknown and running in browser — does wasm change anything re AbortController?

tj: in-browser wasm sees the browser's AbortController (via JS glue), so same semantics. server-side wasm runtimes (wasmtime, lucet) might not wire browser APIs — then you need host bindings for aborting. For us browser WASM behaves like frontend code.

maria: also check token refresh retry logic: if you retry by replaying the same `Request` object, some Fetch implementations don't allow reuse of a consumed body. Always create a fresh Request or clone body when retrying.

alex: good call. any advice for propagating a specific abort reason so caller knows whether it was user-cancel vs timeout?

sam: prefer encoding reason into the error you throw after abort handler runs. AbortSignal itself doesn't carry a reason consistently across runtimes. Example pattern:```js
const ctrl = new AbortController();
let abortReason = null;
parent.addEventListener('abort', () => { abortReason = 'parent-cancel'; ctrl.abort(); });
setTimeout(() => { abortReason = 'timeout'; ctrl.abort(); }, timeout);
try { await fetch(url, { signal: ctrl.signal }); }
catch (err) {
  err.abortReason = abortReason;
  throw err;
}
```
This keeps the signal primitive while giving your higher-level API something meaningful.

irina: one more: for streaming retries, close the previous websocket/duplex stream cleanly before spinning up a new one. For SSE/chunked streams, send a small finalizing message if protocol allows. Leaky half-closed sockets are the root cause in one incident we had.

alex: thanks — will refactor to per-request AbortControllers, hook up parent listeners, and clear timers on abort. I'll also add explicit body cancelation and annotate errors with reasons.

maria: ping if you want a quick pair to patch this — I can help test in browser + node. :thumbsup:

alex: appreciate it, will grab you in 15.

